 "wiki": {
  "title": "Pachydactylus atorquatus",
  "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachydactylus_atorquatus",
  "revision": 1354665213,
  "sections": [
   {
    "h": null,
    "level": 1,
    "paras": [
     "Pachydactylus atorquatus, also known commonly as the Augrabies gecko and Good's gecko, is a species of thick-toed gecko, a lizard in the family Gekkonidae. The species is native to southern Africa."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Taxonomy",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "P. atorquatus belongs to the P. weberi species group."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Geographic range",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "P. atorquatus is found in Namibia and South Africa."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Habitat",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "The preferred natural habitat of P. atorquatus is rocky areas in shrubland, at altitudes of 500–800 m (1,600–2,600 ft)."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Description",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "Large for its genus, P. atorquatus may attain a snout-to-vent length (SVL) of 5.0 cm (2.0 in). It is the only species in the weberi group which does not have a pale collar as a hatchling or juvenile, a key character to which the specific name atorquatus refers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Reproduction",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "P. atorquatus is oviparous."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Etymology",
    "level": 2,
    "paras": [
     "The synonym P. goodi was named in honor of herpetologist David Andrew Good."
    ]
   }
  ]
 }
}